<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gebel Sheikh Suleiman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gebel Sheikh Suleiman is a rock outcrop near Wadi Halfa at the Second Cataract of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, which bore a rock relief of around 3000 BCE. The relief shows a captive tied to a boat and fallen enemies beside Egyptian royal symbols, recording one of the earliest Egyptian campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. It was moved to the National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a> flooded the site.</p></div>
